    "content": "Artemis II is a ~10-day crewed flight test of the SLS rocket and Orion spacecraft, scheduled for April 2026, to fly four astronauts around the Moon and back. The mission, detailed in and mapped by NASA in, will test life-support systems, navigation, and manual piloting with a \"figure-8\" free-return trajectory, passing within ~4,100 miles of the lunar surface. Key Flight Plan Phases: Launch & Earth Orbit (Day 1): The SLS rocket lifts off from Kennedy Space Center, placing Orion in an initial high-Earth orbit. The crew performs systems checks and manual proximity operations. Translunar Injection (Day 2): Orion fires its engines to leave Earth's orbit and enter a \"free-return\" trajectory toward the Moon. Lunar Flyby (Days 3-6): The spacecraft travels for roughly four days to reach the Moon, performing trajectory corrections, before swinging around the far side. Return to Earth (Days 7-10): The crew returns to Earth, performs a radiation shelter demo, and splashes down in the Pacific Ocean. Key Objectives: Validation: First crewed flight to test Orion’s life-support and human-rated systems in deep space. Proximity Ops: The crew will manually pilot the spacecraft near the Interim Cryogenic Propulsion Stage (ICPS) to evaluate handling. Preparation: Paves the way for future Artemis moon landings (Artemis III). For more details on the crew, you can also look at the Artemis II Wikipedia page. Artemis II Wikipedia page",
